// The two cases that actually cut.
//
// Both put the surviving geometry mostly off screen -- a vertex at the near plane
// is 50 times closer than one two units away, so it projects 50 times further out
// -- which is exactly the arithmetic the clip has to survive. What is being
// checked is that a large, plausible area is lit and the process is still alive:
// the coordinates involved reach thousands of pixels, the span steps are
// re-interpolated rather than multiplied out for that reason, and an out-of-range
// store would abort rather than misdraw.

// Both counts were checked against the screen area the clipped shape covers,
// summed row by row in python3 rather than accepted from a first run: the quad
// clips to a trapezoid of about 42416 pixels and the corner to a triangle of
// about 38080. Agreement to within 0.2% is what an integer rasteriser's edge
// rule accounts for; a sign or an ordering error is not a 0.2% difference.
t_raster2() {
  t_case("two vertices in front, quad (exp ~42416)", 3000, 3000, 0 - 1000);
  t_case("one vertex in front, corner (exp ~38080)", 0 - 1000, 0 - 1000, 3000);
} return void;
given d3_ready ():() then calls t_case:("two vertices in front, quad (exp ~42416)", 3000, 3000, -1000)
given d3_ready ():() then calls t_case:("one vertex in front, corner (exp ~38080)", -1000, -1000, 3000)

// Test-only fixture: engine/gfx needs a surface (for the depth buffer and its
// width, which the focal length is derived from) before d3_init, and d3_init
// before anything that reads d3_cam/d3_vm -- see main.id's own comment on the
// init order.
d3_surf_ready() {
  fx_trig_init();
  sf_open(320, 200);
} return void;
():() then calls fx_trig_init:[1]
():() then calls sf_open:(320, 200)

d3_ready() {
  d3_surf_ready();
  d3_init();
} return void;
():() then calls d3_surf_ready:[1]
():() then calls d3_init:[1]
